    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d147-a1-a9-a11">

                <pb facs="0391" n="381" sameAs="frus:frus1864p1/pg381"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Enclosure 11 in No. 9.]</seg></opener>
                <head>Rear-Admiral Sir B. Walker to Sir P. Wodehouse.</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">“Narcissus,” Simon’s
                            Bay,</hi></placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1863-12-29"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >December</hi> 29, 1863.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> Lieutenant Low, the officer belonging to
                    the Confederate States ship of war Alabama, late in charge of the bark called
                    the Tuscaloosa, (properly the <pb facs="0392" n="382" xml:id="pg382"/> Conrad,
                    of Philadelphia,) having sent me a copy of the protest which he has forwarded to
                    your excellency against the detention of that vessel, I think it right to
                    enclose for your information the copy of my letter to Lieutenant Low, explaining
                    the circumstances under which the so-called Tuscaloosa is detained.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>B. W. WALKER.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
